r a contracted amount based on the estimated time needed. Never use appraisers that charge a percentage of the jewelry's value \u2014 this is a scamming technique. Finished.  \n\nResponse 2 - This comprehensive WikiHow article provides detailed guidance on authenticating Rolex watches through multiple verification methods, beginning with auditory and visual examination of the second hand movement where genuine Rolexes produce minimal ticking sounds due to their automatic movements creating smooth sweeping motion at approximately 8 tiny movements per second, contrasting with quartz watches that make distinct \"tick, tick, tick\" sounds and exhibit jerky second hand motion, followed by inspection of the magnification window over the date display which should magnify the date to 2.5x filling nearly the entire window and be perfectly centered rather than appearing glued on imperfectly, and testing the date change mechanism which should switch to the previous date when hands are rolled back to the 6 o'clock position rather than at 12 o'clock, while also assessing the watch's substantial weight from genuine metal and crystal construction, checking for the absence of clear casebacks which are never found on current Rolex models except rare exhibition pieces, examining the high-quality metal construction throughout with no plastic or cheap aluminum components, testing water resistance by submerging the watch briefly in water to verify perfect airtightness, comparing the watch against official Rolex catalog images for exact dial layout and inscription accuracy, locating and inspecting the precisely engraved serial number between the lugs at six o'clock with sharp detailed lettering rather than sandy acid-etched appearance, finding the case reference number with \"ORIG ROLEX DESIGN\" marking at the opposite lugs, searching for the tiny etched crown logo in the crystal at six o'clock using magnification which became standard in the early to mid-2000s, examining the fine etched